<mxCell id="rulesBox" value="6 KEY DESIGN RULES FOR COLLABORATIVE TEXT EDITOR&#10;&#10;1. WEBSOCKET PERSISTENT CONNECTIONS: HTTP polling = 1M req/sec overhead. WebSocket opens ONE connection per user, server pushes updates only when data changes → 100x less traffic, &lt;300ms latency for 50 concurrent editors. Sticky session (IP hash) keeps user on same gateway instance for WebSocket state continuity.&#10;&#10;2. OT (OPERATIONAL TRANSFORMATION) CONFLICT RESOLUTION: User A inserts 'X' at position 5, User B deletes at position 3 — both at version 10. Server assigns sequential versions (A=v11, B arrives late at v10) → OT transforms B's position: delete happened before A's insert → shift B's position by +1 → Both converge to same text. Server-assigned order = single source of truth. CRDT alternative: operations commute (no server needed, works offline), but every character carries unique ID → higher memory overhead. Google Docs = OT. Figma = CRDT.&#10;&#10;3. REDIS CANONICAL COPY WITH TTL: 90% of reads hit Redis (sub-millisecond) instead of S3 (200ms) or replaying 10K ops from PostgreSQL (5 seconds). TTL=30 min for active docs, auto-evict inactive. On cache miss: fetch snapshot from S3 + replay recent ops from op-log → cache result. Cursor positions EPHEMERAL in Redis HASH TTL=5 min — never persisted to DB (100 updates/sec/user × 100 editors = 10K writes/sec, all temporary).&#10;&#10;4. APPEND-ONLY OP-LOG + PERIODIC SNAPSHOTS: Every edit is an immutable operation {type, position, content, server_version} inserted into PostgreSQL op-log (never updated or deleted). Snapshot every 50 ops or 5 min → gzip compress → S3. Fast load: latest snapshot + replay only recent 5-50 ops (~65ms). Without snapshots: replaying 10K ops = 5 seconds. Version history = list of snapshots with timestamps, rollback creates NEW snapshot (never mutates history).&#10;&#10;5. OFFLINE EDITING WITH SYNC-ON-RECONNECT: User types on airplane → ops stored in IndexedDB with last_known_version=42. Network returns → client sends sync_request{last_known_version:42} → server replies with ops 43-50 → client applies server ops first (they happened before offline edits) → OT-transforms local queued ops to account for server changes → sends batch. Most of time seamless. If irreconcilable conflict (two people rewrote same paragraph) → merge modal pops up, same as Git merge conflict.&#10;&#10;6. CURSOR PRESENCE WITH 100ms THROTTLE: Each cursor move fires WebSocket event with {user_id, cursor_pos, selection_range, color}. Throttle to every 100ms (10 updates/sec instead of 100) → 90% traffic reduction. Broadcast to all other editors in that doc. When User B inserts text before User A's cursor, A's cursor position auto-shifts by OT transform so it stays pointing at right word. Cursors stored in Redis HASH cursors:{docId} with TTL=5 min, removed on disconnect. NEVER persist to DB — purely ephemeral real-time state." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e3f2fd;strokeColor=#1976d2;strokeWidth=3;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=12" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="60" y="760" width="1680" height="220" as="geometry"/></mxCell>

<mxCell id="analogy" value="BEGINNER ANALOGY: Collaborative Whiteboard in a Meeting Room&#10;&#10;Imagine 10 people standing around a whiteboard with markers, all writing notes simultaneously. In real life, you can SEE where everyone else is pointing (cursor presence), you naturally avoid writing over each other (OT conflict resolution prevents overwrite), and if someone's hand blocks your view, you shift your marker to the right (position transform). When the board fills up, someone takes a photo (snapshot), erases parts, but keeps the photo in a drawer (S3) so you can refer back to it. If the power goes out mid-meeting (offline), you scribble notes on your notepad (IndexedDB), then when lights return (reconnect), you read what others wrote while you were in the dark, adjust your own notes if they conflict, and add them to the board (sync-on-reconnect). The whiteboard itself is the Redis cache — fast, ephemeral, thrown away after the meeting ends (TTL=30min). The photo archive is S3 (permanent, compressed). The meeting minutes typed up later are the PostgreSQL op-log (immutable record of every word said)." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#fff9c4;strokeColor=#f57f17;strokeWidth=2;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="60" y="1020" width="1680" height="120" as="geometry"/></mxCell>

<mxCell id="scaleNote" value="SCALE TARGETS: 1M concurrent users, 50-100 editors per hot doc, &lt;300ms propagation latency, 1.02M character doc limit, 10K WebSocket connections per gateway instance" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=#ef6c00;fillColor=#fff3e0;align=center;fontSize=11;rounded=1;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="320" y="560" width="920" height="40" as="geometry"/></mxCell>

<mxCell id="capNote" value="CAP: AP (Availability + Partition Tolerance) with Strong Eventual Consistency — all clients with same ops → same final state (convergence guaranteed by OT/CRDT)" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=#7b1fa2;fillColor=#f3e5f5;align=center;fontSize=11;rounded=1" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="320" y="620" width="920" height="40" as="geometry"/></mxCell>

</root></mxGraphModel></diagram></mxfile>
